hibr: bad.sh:2: unquoted-path: rm is handed $f unquoted: a blank or a * in its value makes more paths than meant; write "$f"
hibr: bad.sh:4: for-ls: for over $(ls) splits file names at blanks and globs them; loop over a glob: for f in *
hibr: bad.sh:6: cd-unchecked: cd can fail, and then everything after it runs in the wrong directory; write cd ... || exit
hibr: bad.sh:9: test-unquoted: $x is unquoted in [: if it is empty the test loses a word and errors or is always true; write "$x"
hibr: bad.sh:14: bind-in-a-stage: := in a pipeline stage binds in a child of its own, so the variable is still unset afterwards; bind it on a line of its own, or read the value inside a { ... } stage
hibr: bad.sh:18: unset-quoted-key: unset 'm[$k]' removes the key written there, $ and all, since a quoted subscript is a literal key; write it unquoted with the variable quoted inside, m["$k"]
hibr: bad.sh:21: local-self-ref: $a is read in the same local that assigns it, before it is assigned: put a=... on its own line first
hibr: bad.sh:24: local-masks-status: $? after local x=$(...) is local's own status, always 0, not the command's; declare it first, then assign it on a line of its own
hibr: bad.sh:27: dead-return: ret already ends the function with status 0, so this failing return never runs; for a failure, return without ret
hibr: bad.sh:34: bare-key: ${m[row]}: an unquoted key is looked up as a variable first; if row is the key itself, write ["row"], or declare m -A
status 0
